theory.intro:
A line of best fit can predict values that were not recorded. **Interpolation** predicts inside the range of the recorded data; **extrapolation** predicts outside it. Interpolation is more trustworthy, because the relationship may not continue beyond the data.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\usetikzlibrary{arrows.meta}
\draw[-{Stealth}] (0,0) -- (9.6,0);
\draw[-{Stealth}] (0,0) -- (0,5.8);
\node at (10.0,0) {$x$};
\node at (0,6.2) {$y$};
\draw[dashed] (0.6,0.9) -- (3.0,2.10);
\draw[thick] (3.0,2.10) -- (6.4,3.80);
\draw[dashed] (6.4,3.80) -- (8.6,4.90);
\foreach \p in {(3.1,2.35),(3.5,2.10),(3.9,2.75),(4.3,2.60),(4.7,3.15),(5.1,2.90),(5.5,3.55),(5.9,3.30),(6.3,3.95)} {\fill \p circle (2.4pt);}
\draw[dotted] (3.0,0) -- (3.0,2.10);
\draw[dotted] (6.4,0) -- (6.4,3.80);
\draw[{Stealth}-{Stealth}] (0.15,-0.45) -- (2.90,-0.45);
\draw[{Stealth}-{Stealth}] (3.10,-0.45) -- (6.30,-0.45);
\draw[{Stealth}-{Stealth}] (6.50,-0.45) -- (9.30,-0.45);
\node at (1.5,-1.05) {Extrapolation};
\node at (4.7,-1.05) {Interpolation};
\node at (7.9,-1.05) {Extrapolation};
\node at (7.3,5.3) {line of best fit};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

foundation.question_text:
A gardener recorded a plant's height each week from week $1$ to week $8$, and drew a line of best fit.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, xscale=0.65, yscale=0.20]
\draw[->] (0,0)--(11.5,0);
\draw[->] (0,0)--(0,46);
\foreach \x in {0,2,4,6,8,10} {\node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (\x,0) {$\x$};}
\foreach \y in {5,10,15,20,25,30,35} {\node[left,font=\scriptsize] at (-0.3,\y) {$\y$};}
\node[left,font=\scriptsize] at (-0.3,0) {$0$};
\foreach \p in {(1,8),(2,12),(3,13),(4,18),(5,19),(6,24),(7,25),(8,30)} {\fill \p circle (2.2pt);}
\draw[thick,blue] (1,8)--(8,29);
\node[rotate=90,font=\scriptsize] at (-1.9,23) {Height (cm)};
\node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (5.5,-5) {Weeks since planting};
\node[font=\small] at (5.5,43) {Plant height vs weeks};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
Use the line of best fit to predict the plant's height at week $4$.

